Freeze investigation, real root cause found (docs/load-test-modules.md,
docs/kernel-boot-debugging.md "The real root cause, confirmed"): a specific
CPU core stops responding to EVERYTHING, including a real per-CPU NMI
backtrace request (sysrq's arch_trigger_cpumask_backtrace), under memory
pressure - confirmed twice independently (CPU 5 in an earlier crash, CPU 6
in a later one, both times triggered via cross-CPU synchronization
primitives used by memory reclaim, specifically lru_add_drain_all() called
by khugepaged).

The owner correctly pushed back on an earlier "this may be unfixable"
conclusion: stock Samsung Android and custom Android ROMs (same downstream
kernel lineage, different userspace) do NOT exhibit this freeze under heavy
load on this exact physical hardware - proving the silicon itself is fine.
This had to be something mainline does differently from downstream, not a
hardware defect.

Confirmed directly via dmesg on this device:

    psci: OSI mode supported.
    [Firmware Bug]: failed to set PC mode: -3
    CPUidle PSCI: Initialized CPU PM domain topology using OSI mode

This kernel is running PSCI OS-initiated (OSI) mode because this device's
firmware refuses platform-coordinated (PC) mode - not a guess, a literal
"[Firmware Bug]" log line from the kernel's own PSCI probe. Research (a
sibling porting project search plus direct mailing-list archive fetches,
verified against the actual raw patch text, not an AI summary) found a
real, unmerged (as of the source kernel version this project is based on,
Linux v7.2) upstream fix series specifically for genpd/cpuidle-psci
behaviour under OSI mode:

  "[PATCH v3 0/5] pmdomain/cpuidle-psci: Fix behaviours for CPU PM domains"
  Ulf Hansson, 2026-09-07, reported by Qualcomm's own Maulik Shah
  (lore.kernel.org/all/20260811-domain_off_ss3-v1-0-6a0a0fc023f5@oss.qualcomm.com/)

The bug: when the PM domain/topology is registered through genpd, it's not
yet known whether the corresponding CPUs are online, so genpd may assume a
domain is powered ON when it may not be - or vice versa. Under OSI mode
this is worse: a non-CPU device sharing the same PM domain can't trust the
domain's reported status at all. The fix initializes CPU PM domains as
powered OFF and in the deepest idle state under OSI, using a new
GENPD_FLAG_POWER_UNKNOWN flag to preserve correct ->sync_state() behaviour,
and moves cpuidle-psci's own init earlier (subsys_initcall instead of
device_initcall) so this happens before other device_initcall-level
drivers try to attach to a CPU domain that isn't ready.

This backports the minimal core of that fix (patches 3, 4, and 5 of 5 -
the genpd flag/logic and the two cpuidle-psci changes; patches 1-2 address
a separate, unrelated problem: letting a *non-CPU* device sharing a CPU
domain force it awake via IPI, which this device's own confirmed bug
doesn't need). Verified against this project's own v7.2 tree: all context
lines matched exactly, no fuzz. The one known caveat in the v3 series
(Dhruva Gole, 2026-09-11: GENPD_FLAG_POWER_UNKNOWN implying
GENPD_FLAG_RPM_ALWAYS_ON breaks pm_genpd_init() under CONFIG_PREEMPT_RT)
does not apply here - confirmed via `grep CONFIG_PREEMPT_RT .config`
returning nothing; this project's kernel doesn't use PREEMPT_RT.

Sources (raw patch text fetched and verified directly, not AI-summarized):
  https://ratatoskr.run/linux-pm/2026/09/17524643 (patch 3/5)
  https://ratatoskr.run/linux-pm/2026/09/17524644 (patch 4/5, the main fix)
  https://ratatoskr.run/linux-pm/2026/09/17524642 (patch 5/5)

Status: built and flashed to real hardware for testing (2026-09-21). Not
yet confirmed whether it resolves the freeze - update this file's status
once tested (docs/load-test-modules.md has the test log).

diff --git a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci-domain.c b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci-domain.c
index b9e4ad7d4..4d8c63d32 100644
--- a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci-domain.c
+++ b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci-domain.c
@@ -68,7 +68,8 @@ static int psci_pd_init(struct device_node *np, bool use_osi)
 	 */
 	if (use_osi) {
 		pd->power_off = psci_pd_power_off;
-		pd->flags |= GENPD_FLAG_ACTIVE_WAKEUP;
+		pd->flags |= GENPD_FLAG_ACTIVE_WAKEUP | GENPD_FLAG_POWER_UNKNOWN;
+		pd->state_idx = pd->state_count ? pd->state_count - 1 : 0;
 		if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_PREEMPT_RT))
 			pd->flags |= GENPD_FLAG_RPM_ALWAYS_ON;
 	} else {
@@ -78,7 +79,7 @@ static int psci_pd_init(struct device_node *np, bool use_osi)
 	/* Use governor for CPU PM domains if it has some states to manage. */
 	pd_gov = pd->states ? &pm_domain_cpu_gov : NULL;

-	ret = pm_genpd_init(pd, pd_gov, false);
+	ret = pm_genpd_init(pd, pd_gov, use_osi);
 	if (ret)
 		goto free_pd_prov;

